Reading a regional summary
The original spreadsheets name each region together with its states, for example “Region 1: Connecticut, Maine, …”. The original code split that text into seven state columns and pivoted it long, so that it could be joined to a Census shapefile. sRtr’s UNOS_regions_sf already records each state’s region, so only the “Region N” part is needed. Proportions are multiplied by 100, as in the original code.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Read one study's regional summary spreadsheet. The original spreadsheets
# name each region as "Region 1: Connecticut, Maine, ...", so only the
# "Region N" part is kept; sRtr already knows which states each region holds.
read_region_summary &lt;- function(path, percent_columns = character()) {
  read_excel(path) |&gt;
    clean_names() |&gt;
    mutate(
      region = str_extract(region, "^Region \\d+"),
      across(all_of(percent_columns), \(x) 100 * x)
    )
}

Synthetic values
When a regional spreadsheet is missing, this makes a random value for each region, so the code still runs. The numbers mean nothing.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Synthetic stand-in for a regional summary, used when the real spreadsheet is
# not available. The values are random and mean nothing.
make_synthetic_region_summary &lt;- function(measures, max_value = 15, seed = 1) {
  set.seed(seed)
  summary &lt;- tibble::tibble(region = str_c("Region ", 1:11))
  for (measure in measures) {
    summary[[measure]] &lt;- round(runif(11, min = 0, max = max_value), 1)
  }
  summary
}

Joining values to the map
Every state takes its region’s values. The original code used inner_join(), which silently drops a region whose name is spelled differently. This function stops instead and names the missing region.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Attach a regional summary to the state polygons. Every state takes its
# region's values. Stops if any region is missing from the summary.
join_region_values &lt;- function(region_states, region_summary) {
  missing_regions &lt;- setdiff(unique(region_states$Region), region_summary$region)
  if (length(missing_regions) &gt; 0) {
    stop(
      "The regional summary has no row for: ",
      str_flatten_comma(sort(missing_regions))
    )
  }
  region_states |&gt;
    left_join(
      region_summary,
      by = join_by(Region == region),
      relationship = "many-to-one"
    )
}

Choosing the transplant centers
The HRSA layer that get_hrsa_transplant_centers() downloads has one row for each center and organ, so a center that transplants five organs appears five times. The function keeps centers that list the organ, then one row per center code (OTC_CD). It then applies the original maps’ rule for pediatric centers (analysis plan section 5).


Click to show/hide R Code
# Keep centers that list the organ, one row per center, excluding pediatric
# centers by name. The HRSA layer has one row per center and organ, so
# distinct() is needed or each center is drawn several times.
select_adult_centers &lt;- function(centers, organ, adult_exceptions, crs) {
  centers |&gt;
    filter(str_detect(Service_Lst, str_c("\\b", organ, "\\b"))) |&gt;
    distinct(OTC_CD, .keep_all = TRUE) |&gt;
    filter(!str_detect(OTC_NM, "Children") | OTC_NM %in% adult_exceptions) |&gt;
    st_transform(crs)
}
